' 18_extensions.ds — everything Directive has that VBScript does not.
'
' Directive's rule is "mirror VBScript": when behaviour is ambiguous it does what
' classic VBScript does. This file is the deliberate exception list — the places
' where Directive adds something, and why. Everything else (Option Explicit,
' Class, Err, RegExp, Dictionary, the 100+ builtins, #date# literals, bracket
' identifiers like [End]) behaves exactly as VBScript does and is not listed here.
'
' Runs on every platform: the Windows-only pieces are guarded and report instead
' of failing.
Option Explicit
On Error Resume Next

Directive.Echo "=============================================================="
Directive.Echo " Directive extensions — not present in VBScript"
Directive.Echo "=============================================================="

' -- 1. Host object and object creation ---------------------------------------
' The host object is always "Directive". Two separate creation verbs:
'   New          -> native engine objects, cross-platform, no COM
'   CreateObject -> real COM/ActiveX, Windows only (unchanged from VBScript)
Directive.Echo ""
Directive.Echo "1. Host object + New vs CreateObject"
Directive.Echo "   host object:  Directive (" & TypeName(Directive) & ")"
Directive.Echo "   script:       " & Directive.ScriptName
Directive.Echo "   New          = native objects (cross-platform, COM-free)"
Directive.Echo "   CreateObject = real COM/ActiveX (Windows only)"

' -- 2. ds* constants ----------------------------------------------------------
' Every vb* constant was replaced by a ds* one. Porting is a plain vb -> ds
' substitution on constant names; there are no vb* aliases left.
Directive.Echo ""
Directive.Echo "2. ds* constants replace vb*"
Directive.Echo "   dsCrLf, dsTab, dsYesNo, dsInformation, dsTextCompare, ..."
Directive.Echo "   dsString = " & dsString & ", dsTextCompare = " & dsTextCompare

' -- 3. New List ---------------------------------------------------------------
' VBScript has arrays and Dictionary but no list. This one is ordered, 0-based,
' and grows without ReDim Preserve.
Directive.Echo ""
Directive.Echo "3. New List — ordered collection VBScript lacks"
Dim L : Set L = New List
L.Add "pear" : L.Add "apple" : L.Add "fig"
L.Sort
Directive.Echo "   sorted:   " & Join(L.ToArray(), ", ")
L.Reverse
Directive.Echo "   reversed: " & Join(L.ToArray(), ", ") & "   (Count=" & L.Count & ")"

' -- 4. New Console ------------------------------------------------------------
' Colours, cursor control, screen clearing and single-key input. VBScript has no
' console API at all, which is why batch scripts shell out to `color` and `cls`.
' See 17_console.ds for the full tour.
Directive.Echo ""
Directive.Echo "4. New Console — colours, cursor, Cls, ReadKey, Pause"
Dim c : Set c = New Console
Directive.Echo "   attached=" & c.Attached & "  size=" & c.Width & "x" & c.Height
c.Write "   coloured output: "
c.Write "green", dsBrightGreen
c.Write " / "
c.WriteLine "red on white", dsRed, dsWhite

' -- 5. Include ----------------------------------------------------------------
' Parse-time file inclusion, resolved relative to the including file and applied
' once per path (safe for diamond and circular includes). VBScript has no
' equivalent outside .wsf job files. See 12_include.ds.
Directive.Echo ""
Directive.Echo "5. Include ""lib/strutil.ds"" — parse-time, no .wsf needed"

' -- 6. New Shell --------------------------------------------------------------
' A COM-free rewrite of the shell surface: Run/Exec/Environment work everywhere,
' registry and special folders are Windows-only.
Directive.Echo ""
Directive.Echo "6. New Shell — COM-free process/env/registry access"
Dim sh : Set sh = New Shell
Dim tmp : tmp = sh.ExpandEnvironmentStrings("%PATH%")
Directive.Echo "   PATH is " & Len(tmp) & " chars"

' -- 7. New Sound --------------------------------------------------------------
' WAV combining is pure file I/O and works everywhere; playback and Beep need
' the Windows build.
Directive.Echo ""
Directive.Echo "7. New Sound — WAV combining (all platforms) + playback (Windows)"
Dim snd : Set snd = New Sound
Err.Clear
snd.Beep 440, 80
If Err.Number <> 0 Then
    Directive.Echo "   Beep: " & Err.Description
    Err.Clear
Else
    Directive.Echo "   Beep: played"
End If

' -- 8. Desktop automation ----------------------------------------------------
' AutoIt-style input and screen access, deliberately split into single-purpose
' objects rather than one catch-all. Windows-only.
Directive.Echo ""
Directive.Echo "8. New Mouse / Screen / Keyboard / Clipboard — Windows-only"
Dim scr
Err.Clear
Set scr = New Screen
If Err.Number <> 0 Then
    Directive.Echo "   " & Err.Description
    Err.Clear
Else
    Directive.Echo "   desktop is " & scr.Width & "x" & scr.Height & " px"
End If
' Keyboard.Send takes an AutoIt-style raw-mode second argument:
'   Send "{ENTER}"     -> presses Enter
'   Send "{ENTER}", 1  -> types the eight literal characters
Directive.Echo "   Keyboard.Send(keys, 1) types braces literally instead of parsing them"

' -- 9. Unicode text files ----------------------------------------------------
' UTF-8 by default, with UTF-16LE/BE detected on read via BOM and forced on write
' through CreateTextFile(path, True) / OpenTextFile(..., -1), including surrogate
' pairs for emoji. See 15_unicode.ds.
Directive.Echo ""
Directive.Echo "9. FileSystem — UTF-8 default, UTF-16LE/BE with BOM detection"

' -- 10. One source, two hosts ------------------------------------------------
' directive.exe is the console host and directivew.exe the windowed one, both
' built from a single self-contained C++17 source with no external dependencies.
Directive.Echo ""
Directive.Echo "10. Single static binary — directive.exe (console) / directivew.exe (GUI)"
Directive.Echo "    No Windows Script Host, no COM registration, no .dll to install."

Directive.Echo ""
Directive.Echo "=============================================================="

' --- PAUSE (batch equivalent): only waits when this script owns the console,
' so a double-click stays readable while piped/prompt/GUI runs are unaffected.
Dim keepOpen : Set keepOpen = New Console : keepOpen.KeepOpen
